WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.250
- Let's close out with
one last musical story

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:03.450
I hope will bring you some joy.

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:04.491
This is what I'm liking.

00:00:04.491 --> 00:00:07.020
(cheerful energetic music)
(audience clapping)

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:10.230
All hour we have seen just how
transformative music can be.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.200
For the past 12 years, girls
in Nebraska have learned

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.530
that firsthand taking part in
a program that uses pop music

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:19.050
as a tool to instill self-assurance.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.910
It's called Omaha Girls Rock,

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:22.650
and we have the executive director

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.930
on the line right now watching on KETV.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.051
Everybody please say hello to Halley.

00:00:27.051 --> 00:00:27.935
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:00:27.935 --> 00:00:29.061
Hi.

00:00:29.061 --> 00:00:30.360
- Hi.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:31.193
- What's up?

00:00:31.193 --> 00:00:33.060
So tell us more about the program.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.920
- Oh my God, Kelly, I would love to.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.950
So our mission at Omaha Girls
Rock is to empower youth

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:41.070
to use their voice, and
we do that through music.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.350
So our organization has
year round programming.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:46.830
We do instrument instruction,
we do afterschool programming,

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.770
but the biggest programming that we have,

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.260
and the most popular,
are our summer camps,

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.600
where in five days our youth,

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.610
with or without musical experience,

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.520
are learning an
instrument, forming a band,

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.370
writing an original song,
and, on the fifth day,

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.800
performing when they headline a showcase

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.570
at a local music venue.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:09.990
So it is a phenomenal organization,

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.810
and it's my honor to be
able to be here with you.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.520
- Oh my gosh, it sounds incredible.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.170
I'd have loved to be a part
of this when I was a kid.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.140
So you use popular music
to create conversations

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:19.973
with the kiddos.

00:01:19.973 --> 00:01:21.030
So how do you do that?

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.350
- I think it's the easiest way

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.990
for our teaching artists
to begin relationships

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:26.550
or start forming those relationships

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.200
with the youth that we mentor.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.780
So our youth come to us
with a variety of genres

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:31.890
that they're interested in

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.350
and that is where we begin our learning.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:35.610
That is where we, you know,

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:39.510
instill the skills needed to
learn the guitar, for example.

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:42.750
But we also are using a
variety of those genres

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:46.380
and artists who are, you
know, local, national,

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.340
international, to talk
about how to take up space

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:54.060
and how to live in the
intersections of your identities

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:59.060
because there is not just
one way to be successful.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:02.340
We use Lizzo, for example,
and we talk about, you know,

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.430
beginning with the flute, is
this a hip hop instrument?

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:07.260
And our youth, you know,

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.480
understand that those preconceived notions

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.970
about what a pop star is supposed to be,

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:16.970
what musicians rock, that is
up to each of us to decide.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.350
So we are all about that self-empowerment

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.630
of learning the instrument
and also learning

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:25.170
to be powerful exactly as you
are, regardless of body size

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:27.993
regardless of race,
regardless of gender identity.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.930
Releasing ourself from those standards

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:35.370
and truly just standing on
those stages in our truth.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:36.203
- Man, it's incredible.

00:02:36.203 --> 00:02:38.190
It's like you're helping
these kids find their power

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:39.780
and their purpose, like, all at once.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.310
It's really, really cool.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:43.200
Thank you so much for joining us,

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.872
and also I'm liking your post right now.

00:02:45.872 --> 00:02:46.705
(post chiming)
(audience clapping)

00:02:46.705 --> 00:02:47.910
- [Halley] Thank you.
- All right.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:48.743
Well, that's not all.

00:02:48.743 --> 00:02:51.210
Our season long partner,
Pilot Pen, makers of FriXion,

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:53.160
the go to erasable pen of
"The Kelly Clarkson Show,"

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.400
want to celebrate those creating
fearlessly just like you.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.160
So they're gonna write a
check to Omaha Girls Rock

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:00.609
for a thousand dollars just to help out.

00:03:00.609 --> 00:03:02.910
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.670
- Amazing. Thank you so much.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.800
- Thank you so much, Halley,
and thank you so much.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:09.120
A big thank you to all our guests today:

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.100
Seal, Niall Horan, Shania Twain,
Ava Max, and Grace Gaustad.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.233
Y'all have a great day, and if
it's not, try and change it.

